    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit: creating an owner is now rejected with **429** once 100 or more owners already have a `registrationDate` of today.\n\nFollowing the existing `EnsureCityCapacity` pattern, I added four files and one wiring change:\n\n1. **`EnsureDailyOwnerLimit.java`** (function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and throws `TooManyOwnersTodayException` when the count is \u2265 100.\n\n2. **`TooManyOwnersTodayException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception (extends `Exception`) so it surfaces on the `throws` clause for escalation matching.\n\n3. **`TooManyOwnersTodayExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching the other capacity handlers.\n\n4. **`...TooManyOwnersTodayException.yml`** (escalation) \u2014 routes the exception to the handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyLimit` step between `ensureCityCapacity` and `build`, so the check runs in the validation phase (before the transaction) alongside the other quota checks.\n\nThe pre-existing acceptance test `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (create 100, expect the 101st to return 429) now passes, and the full `mvn test` run is green.",
